# Scripts that run on Linux must have LF line endings. With CRLF the kernel cannot find
# the interpreter and fails with "bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M", an error that does not name
# its cause. Git already keeps them as LF in the index, but that guarantee depends on the
# configuration of whoever checks them out: this line makes it a property of the repository.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The workflows too: the "run:" blocks hold shell scripts that run on Linux, and release.yml
# contains a heredoc. A stray carriage return would stop the closing delimiter from matching
# the opening one, and the script would fail in a way that does not name its cause - the
# same failure already seen in the files under packaging/linux/debian.
.github/workflows/*.yml text eol=lf

# The rule applies to the DIRECTORY, not to individual files, and that is a fix: the list
# of file names that used to be here covered the seven files that existed that day, and the
# next day three more arrived - two man pages and the lintian overrides - which were
# silently left out. Everything in this directory ends up inside the .deb and is read by
# Linux tools: a stray carriage return in lintian-overrides makes the package analysis
# fail, and in a man page it shows up on screen.
packaging/linux/debian/** text eol=lf

# PowerShell scripts stay CRLF: that is the convention on their platform, and there it does no harm.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Binaries: git must not touch them, either to convert line endings or to diff them.
*.ico binary
*.png binary
*.msi binary
*.deb binary
